# Constrained types → enums

When a value may only be one of a **closed, known set** of strings or numbers, model it as an
**enum** (or a Prisma `enum` in the database layer). Do not use `type X = 'a' | 'b'`, bare
`string`, or magic literals scattered in comparisons.

## When this applies

- Props, function args, return types, route/query params after validation
- Roles, scopes, statuses, steps in wizards, adapter kinds, event names
- Shared constants compared with `===` in more than one place

## When not to force an enum

- **Discriminated unions** for shape (`{ ok: true; data } | { ok: false; error }`) — keep as union types
- **Open-ended** strings (email, slug, free text, URLs)
- **Generated / external** types you do not own (narrow at the boundary, then map into your enum)

## TypeScript

- Prefer **`export enum Name { ... }`** with **string values** that match wire/JSON/DB literals
- Use the enum in types **and** at runtime (`Name.STAFF`, not `'staff'` literals in app code)
- Validate unknown input once, then assign to the enum; use `Object.values(Name)` for guards if needed
- One enum per concept; do not duplicate the same closed set as both enum and union type

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — union drifts, literals hide in switches
export type AccessAppScope = 'staff' | 'partner' | 'public'
if (scope === 'staff') { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
export enum AccessAppScope {
  Staff = 'staff',
  Partner = 'partner',
  Public = 'public',
}
if (scope === AccessAppScope.Staff) { ... }
```

## Database

- **Website API (Prisma):** define closed sets as Prisma `enum` in schema; use generated enum types in TS
- **Platform Postgres (SQL migrations):** use `CHECK (... IN (...))` or Postgres enums; mirror in TS with a matching string enum at the service boundary

## Refactors

When touching code that still uses a string union for a closed set, convert to an enum in the same
change (or a focused follow-up) instead of adding more literal strings.
